The `\rowcolor` command in LaTeX is used to change the background color of a row in a table. This command is part of the `colortbl` package, which provides various commands for adding color to tables.

Here's a basic example of how to use `\rowcolor`:

1. First, ensure you include the `colortbl` package in the preamble of your LaTeX document:
   ```latex
   \usepackage{colortbl}
   ```

2. Then, you can use the `\rowcolor` command within your table environment to color a row. Here is an example:

   ```latex
   \documentclass{article}
   \usepackage{colortbl}

   \begin{document}

   \begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
     \hline
     \rowcolor{yellow}
     Header 1 & Header 2 & Header 3 \\
     \hline
     Row 1, Col 1 & Row 1, Col 2 & Row 1, Col 3 \\
     \rowcolor{blue!20}
     Row 2, Col 1 & Row 2, Col 2 & Row 2, Col 3 \\
     \hline
   \end{tabular}

   \end{document}
   ```

In this example:
- The first row (headers) is colored yellow.
- The third row is colored with a light blue (`blue!20`).

You can specify any color you want using predefined colors like `red`, `green`, `blue`, etc., or you can mix colors using the `xcolor` package. For example, `blue!20` means 20% blue mixed with white.

To use more advanced color mixing, you might want to include the `xcolor` package as well:

```latex
\usepackage[table]{xcolor}
```

This allows you to define custom colors and use more complex color specifications.

Here is an example using the `xcolor` package:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[table]{xcolor}

\begin{document}

\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
  \hline
  \rowcolor{yellow}
  Header 1 & Header 2 & Header 3 \\
  \hline
  Row 1, Col 1 & Row 1, Col 2 & Row 1, Col 3 \\
  \rowcolor{blue!20}
  Row 2, Col 1 & Row 2, Col 2 & Row 2, Col 3 \\
  \hline
\end{tabular}

\end{document}
```

In this example, the `xcolor` package is used to provide more flexibility in color definitions.

